    "# CS229 L06 — Bias-Variance Tradeoff, Regularization & ML Advice\n",
    "\n",
    "**Video:** Spring 2026 · [YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=llnEgyyuYkQ)  \n",
    "**Instructor:** Chris Re  \n",
    "**Topics:** Overfitting · Underfitting · Bias-Variance Decomposition · Ridge Regression · Modern Regularization · Double Descent · Train/Dev/Test Splits · Cross Validation · Hyperband\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "> 📌 *Lecture:* \"Today we're really wrestling with this central question in machine learning: given a finite noisy sample, how do we pick a model that will generalize? This is what motivated both statistical theory and what we do now.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## Roadmap\n",
    "\n",
    "| Section | Key Idea |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| 1. Overfitting vs. Underfitting | Pictures first — three polynomial cases |\n",
    "| 2. Bias-Variance Decomposition | Formal derivation: error = noise + bias² + variance |\n",
    "| 3. Ridge Regression | L2 penalty → shift eigenvalues → lower variance |\n",
    "| 4. Modern Regularization | Dropout, weight decay, data augmentation, layer norm |\n",
    "| 5. Train/Dev/Test Splits | Why you must never peek at the test set |\n",
    "| 6. K-Fold Cross Validation | Data-efficient model selection |\n",
    "| 7. Hyperband | Compute-efficient hyperparameter search (successive halving) |\n",
    "| 8. Double Descent | Modern twist: overparameterized models can still generalize |\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Overfitting vs. Underfitting\n",
    "\n",
    "### Setup\n",
    "\n",
    "There exists a **true function** $h^*(x)$ in the world (e.g., a quadratic) that generates data:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "y^{(i)} = h^*(x^{(i)}) + \\varepsilon^{(i)}, \\quad \\varepsilon^{(i)} \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, \\sigma^2)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "We never observe $h^*$ — only the noisy samples $\\{(x^{(i)}, y^{(i)})\\}$. Our goal: learn $h_S$ from training set $S$ such that $h_S$ generalizes to new data.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Key mental shift for this lecture:** treat the training set $S$ itself as a random draw from the data-generating process. Different draws → different $h_S$. We want $h_S$ to be stable across draws.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Three cases\n",
    "\n",
    "| Case | Model class | Symptom | Name |\n",
    "|---|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Too simple | Line fitting a quadratic | Always makes systematic error, no matter how much data | **High Bias / Underfitting** |\n",
    "| Just right | Correct degree polynomial | Fits well; stable across different training sets | **Good fit** |\n",
    "| Too complex | Degree-9 polynomial on 10 points | Fits training set perfectly but wildly unstable | **High Variance / Overfitting** |\n",
    "\n",
    "> 📌 *Lecture:* \"In the high variance case, the model is fitting noise, not signal. The true signal is this nice smooth parabola and it's fitting all the little noise. As a result, it's jumping around wildly.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "### Interpolation\n",
    "\n",
    "**Interpolation** = fitting the training set with zero loss. Classically, an interpolating model was assumed to overfit catastrophically. The *double descent* result (Section 8) shows this isn't always true for modern models.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "> 🎯 **Interview:** What is the difference between high bias and high variance?\n",
    "> \n",
    "> **A:** High bias (underfitting): the model class is too simple to capture the true function. Error is systematic — no amount of additional data fixes it. Example: fitting a line to quadratic data. High variance (overfitting): the model class is too expressive and fits the noise in each training set. The learned function changes dramatically with different training samples. The fix for bias is a richer model class; the fix for variance is regularization or more data.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "from numpy.polynomial import polynomial as P\n",
    "\n",
    "np.random.seed(0)\n",
    "\n",
    "# True function: quadratic\n",
    "def h_star(x):\n",
    "    return 0.5 * x**2 - x + 1\n",
    "\n",
    "x_true = np.linspace(-2, 3, 200)\n",
    "n_samples = 12\n",
    "sigma = 0.6\n",
    "\n",
    "# Generate 3 different training sets\n",
    "sets = []\n",
    "for _ in range(3):\n",
    "    x = np.random.uniform(-2, 3, n_samples)\n",
    "    y = h_star(x) + np.random.normal(0, sigma, n_samples)\n",
    "    sets.append((x, y))\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(3, 3, figsize=(14, 10))\n",
    "degrees = [1, 2, 9]  # underfitting, just right, overfitting\n",
    "titles = ['Degree 1 (Underfit, High Bias)', 'Degree 2 (Just Right)', 'Degree 9 (Overfit, High Variance)']\n",
    "colors = ['C0', 'C1', 'C2']\n",
    "\n",
    "for col, (deg, title) in enumerate(zip(degrees, titles)):\n",
    "    for row, (x_tr, y_tr) in enumerate(sets):\n",
    "        ax = axes[row][col]\n",
    "        # Fit polynomial\n",
    "        coeffs = np.polyfit(x_tr, y_tr, deg)\n",
    "        y_fit = np.polyval(coeffs, x_true)\n",
    "        # Clip extreme oscillations for visibility\n",
    "        y_fit = np.clip(y_fit, -5, 10)\n",
    "        ax.plot(x_true, h_star(x_true), 'k--', linewidth=1.5, label='True h*' if row == 0 else '')\n",
    "        ax.plot(x_true, y_fit, colors[col], linewidth=2, label=f'Fitted (deg {deg})' if row == 0 else '')\n",
    "        ax.scatter(x_tr, y_tr, c='black', s=25, zorder=5)\n",
    "        ax.set_ylim(-4, 8)\n",
    "        ax.set_title(f'S{row+1}: {title}' if row == 0 else f'S{row+1}', fontsize=9)\n",
    "        ax.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\n",
    "\n",
    "axes[0][0].legend(fontsize=8)\n",
    "plt.suptitle('Overfitting vs. Underfitting Across 3 Training Sets', fontsize=13, y=1.01)\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Left: line always misses the curve — systematic error regardless of training set (Bias)\")\n",
    "print(\"Center: quadratic fits consistently — stable across S1, S2, S3\")\n",
    "print(\"Right: degree-9 wildly changes per training set — fitting noise (Variance)\")"
   ]
  },
