    "## 3. Ridge Regression (L2 Regularization)\n",
    "\n",
    "### The regularized objective\n",
    "\n",
    "Standard least squares minimizes:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "J(\\theta) = \\|X\\theta - y\\|^2\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Ridge regression adds an L2 penalty:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "J_{\\text{ridge}}(\\theta) = \\|X\\theta - y\\|^2 + \\rho \\|\\theta\\|^2\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "**Intuition:** $\\theta$ shouldn't be \"too big\". If a large $\\theta_j$ is needed to fit the data, it must be worth it. The penalty $\\rho$ sets the exchange rate.\n",
    "\n",
    "| $\\rho$ value | Effect |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| $\\rho = 0$ | Plain least squares |\n",
    "| $\\rho \\to \\infty$ | $\\theta \\to 0$ (predicting the mean) |\n",
    "| Good $\\rho$ | Balanced tradeoff |\n",
    "\n",
    "### Closed-form solution\n",
    "\n",
    "Take the derivative, set to zero:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\hat{\\theta}_{\\text{ridge}} = (X^T X + \\rho I)^{-1} X^T y\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Compare to plain least squares: $(X^T X)^{-1} X^T y$.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why it reduces variance: eigenvalue view\n",
    "\n",
    "Let $\\lambda_1, \\ldots, \\lambda_d$ be the eigenvalues of $X^T X$. Adding $\\rho I$ shifts all eigenvalues to $\\lambda_i + \\rho$.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Problem:** if $\\lambda_i$ is near zero (near-singular $X^T X$), inverting it amplifies noise enormously — tiny changes in $X$ or $y$ produce wild swings in $\\hat{\\theta}$.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Ridge fix:** $\\lambda_i + \\rho \\geq \\rho > 0$ — eigenvalues bounded away from zero. The inversion is stable. This directly reduces variance.\n",
    "\n",
    "> 📌 *Lecture:* \"The variance of $\\hat{\\theta}$ involves eigenvalues that shrink from the plain version by $\\lambda_i$ to roughly this expression in ridge. A small value $\\lambda_i$ has huge variance. By adding $\\rho$, I bound it away from zero so everything is large enough — a better, more well-behaved optimization and a lower variance model.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why regularization works in the underdetermined case ($n < d$)\n",
    "\n",
    "When $n < d$: $X^T X$ is not full rank. There exist infinitely many $\\theta$'s that achieve zero training loss. Any $\\theta + v$ for $v$ in $\\text{null}(X)$ gives the same predictions.\n",
    "\n",
    "Ridge selects the **minimum-norm solution** — it penalizes any component in the null space, forcing it toward zero. This is the geometrically simplest solution and typically generalizes best.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Bias introduced\n",
    "\n",
    "Ridge trades a **small bias increase** for a **large variance reduction**:\n",
    "\n",
    "- $\\hat{\\theta}$ is shrunk toward zero → predictions are pulled toward zero → small systematic error introduced\n",
    "- But the stability gain (variance reduction) far outweighs this in practice\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "> 🎯 **Interview:** Why does ridge regression reduce variance?\n",
    "> \n",
    "> **A:** Ridge adds $\\rho I$ to $X^TX$ before inverting. This shifts all eigenvalues from $\\lambda_i$ to $\\lambda_i + \\rho$, bounding them away from zero. Near-zero eigenvalues in $X^TX$ mean that tiny perturbations in training data produce huge swings in $\\hat{\\theta}$ (high variance). By ensuring all eigenvalues are at least $\\rho$, the inversion is numerically stable and the model is less sensitive to which training set it saw. The cost is a small amount of bias — the solution is shrunk toward zero.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "\n",
    "np.random.seed(7)\n",
    "\n",
    "def h_star(x):\n",
    "    return 0.5 * x**2 - x + 1\n",
    "\n",
    "def poly_features(x, deg):\n",
    "    return np.stack([x**k for k in range(deg + 1)], axis=1)  # (n, deg+1)\n",
    "\n",
    "def fit_ridge(X, y, rho):\n",
    "    d = X.shape[1]\n",
    "    return np.linalg.solve(X.T @ X + rho * np.eye(d), X.T @ y)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Settings\n",
    "deg = 8           # overfitting model class\n",
    "n = 15\n",
    "sigma = 0.6\n",
    "n_trials = 200\n",
    "x_plot = np.linspace(-2, 3, 200)\n",
    "X_plot = poly_features(x_plot, deg)\n",
    "\n",
    "rhos = [0.0, 0.01, 0.1, 1.0, 10.0]\n",
    "colors = ['navy', 'steelblue', 'green', 'orange', 'red']\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, len(rhos), figsize=(16, 4), sharey=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "for ax, rho, color in zip(axes, rhos, colors):\n",
    "    all_preds = []\n",
    "    for _ in range(n_trials):\n",
    "        x_tr = np.random.uniform(-2, 3, n)\n",
    "        y_tr = h_star(x_tr) + np.random.normal(0, sigma, n)\n",
    "        X_tr = poly_features(x_tr, deg)\n",
    "        theta = fit_ridge(X_tr, y_tr, rho)\n",
    "        pred = X_plot @ theta\n",
    "        pred = np.clip(pred, -10, 15)\n",
    "        all_preds.append(pred)\n",
    "        ax.plot(x_plot, pred, color=color, alpha=0.07, linewidth=0.8)\n",
    "    mean_pred = np.mean(all_preds, axis=0)\n",
    "    ax.plot(x_plot, h_star(x_plot), 'k--', linewidth=2, label='True h*')\n",
    "    ax.plot(x_plot, mean_pred, color, linewidth=2.5, label=f'Mean pred')\n",
    "    ax.set_ylim(-5, 10); ax.set_xlim(-2, 3)\n",
    "    ax.set_title(f'ρ = {rho}', fontsize=11)\n",
    "    ax.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\n",
    "\n",
    "axes[0].set_ylabel('y', fontsize=11)\n",
    "plt.suptitle(f'Ridge Regression: Effect of ρ (degree-{deg} polynomial, {n_trials} training sets shown)', fontsize=12)\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"ρ=0: wildly different fits each training set → high variance\")\n",
    "print(\"ρ=10: all fits nearly identical → high bias (pulled toward zero)\")\n",
    "print(\"Sweet spot (ρ≈0.1): stable fits that track the true function\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
