   "source": [
    "## 4. Modern Regularization Techniques\n",
    "\n",
    "The L2 penalty is just one form. In modern deep learning, variance is reduced by a range of techniques that all share the same intuition: **prevent the model from fitting too hard to any particular data point**.\n",
    "\n",
    "> 📌 *Lecture:* \"The most engineer intuition you can get is what you're trying to prevent is the model from fitting into the noise. You make your model more robust by doing nasty things to it.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "| Technique | What it does | Regularization interpretation |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| **L2 / Weight decay** | Add $\\rho\\|\\theta\\|^2$ to loss | Shrinks weights toward zero; discourages large weights |\n",
    "| **L1 / Lasso** | Add $\\rho\\|\\theta\\|_1$ to loss | Promotes sparsity; sets unimportant weights to exactly zero |\n",
    "| **Dropout** | Randomly zero out neurons during training | Equivalent to L2 on the weights (Srivastava et al.) |\n",
    "| **Data augmentation** | Rotate/flip/crop training images | Encodes invariances; model can't overfit to orientation-specific noise |\n",
    "| **Layer norm / Batch norm** | Normalize activations per layer | Prevents runaway large values; implicit regularization |\n",
    "| **Early stopping** | Stop training before convergence | Model hasn't yet memorized the noise |\n",
    "\n",
    "All of these can be formally analyzed using the bias-variance framework — the papers for each technique include such a decomposition.\n",
    "\n",
    "### L1 vs. L2 penalty\n",
    "\n",
    "| | L2 (Ridge) | L1 (Lasso) |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Penalty | $\\rho\\sum_j \\theta_j^2$ | $\\rho\\sum_j |\\theta_j|$ |\n",
    "| Effect on weights | Shrinks all weights uniformly | Sets many weights to exactly 0 |\n",
    "| Closed-form solution | Yes | No (requires iterative methods) |\n",
    "| When to use | Features all potentially relevant | Feature selection; sparse solutions |\n",
    "\n",
    "L1 is convex but not differentiable at 0; requires coordinate descent or subgradient methods. Modern ML defaults to L2 for simplicity and hardware friendliness.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Choosing $\\rho$: it comes from data\n",
    "\n",
    "> 📌 *Lecture:* \"The right ratio depends on the data. They're really hard to know up front. So we fit $\\rho$ from data — this is sometimes called hyperparameter optimization. You have some dev set you're developing on. You try various different models, train on one set, test on another, and see which $\\rho$ does better.\"\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Train / Dev / Test Splits\n",
    "\n",
    "### The data split\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
    "All data\n",
    "├── Train set     → fit model parameters (θ)\n",
    "├── Dev set       → tune hyperparameters (ρ, architecture, etc.) — also called \"validation\"\n",
    "└── Test set      → final evaluation — TOUCH ONLY ONCE\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why not use train error to select models?\n",
    "\n",
    "A high-complexity model can drive train error to zero — it doesn't tell you anything about generalization. You need a held-out set that the model never trained on.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why not use the test set for model selection?\n",
    "\n",
    "Every time you peek at the test set and adjust your model accordingly, information leaks back. You're now implicitly fitting the test set. Your estimated test error becomes optimistic. This is called **adaptive overfitting**.\n",
    "\n",
    "> 📌 *Lecture:* \"Your test set is sacred. Every time you peek at it, you could leak information, an inflated estimate of how well you're doing. Statistical-minded people are very worried about spoiling their test set.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "### Typical splits\n",
    "\n",
    "| Dataset size | Train | Dev | Test |\n",
    "|---|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Small (~1K) | 60% | 20% | 20% |\n",
    "| Large (~1M+) | 98% | 1% | 1% |\n",
    "\n",
    "For large datasets, you need only enough dev/test examples to get stable estimates. The bulk should go to training.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 6. K-Fold Cross Validation\n",
    "\n",
    "### Problem with a fixed dev split\n",
    "\n",
    "Holding out 20% of a small dataset wastes precious training data. Your model sees only 80% of what it could.\n",
    "\n",
    "### K-fold solution\n",
    "\n",
    "Split the data into $K$ equal folds. Train $K$ separate times: each time use $K-1$ folds for training and 1 fold as the dev set. Average the $K$ dev scores.\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
    "K=5 example:\n",
    "Fold 1: [DEV] [TR ] [TR ] [TR ] [TR ]\n",
    "Fold 2: [TR ] [DEV] [TR ] [TR ] [TR ]\n",
    "Fold 3: [TR ] [TR ] [DEV] [TR ] [TR ]\n",
    "Fold 4: [TR ] [TR ] [TR ] [DEV] [TR ]\n",
    "Fold 5: [TR ] [TR ] [TR ] [TR ] [DEV]\n",
    "\n",
    "CV score = mean of 5 dev scores\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "**Key property:** every example is used for training in $K-1$ rounds and for evaluation exactly once. No data is wasted.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Leave-one-out CV (LOOCV):** $K = n$ — each example is the dev set once. Unbiased estimate but $n$ times more compute.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Typical choice:** $K = 5$ or $K = 10$ — good balance of compute and data efficiency.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "from itertools import product\n",
    "\n",
    "def h_star(x):\n",
    "    return 0.5 * x**2 - x + 1\n",
    "\n",
    "def poly_features(x, deg):\n",
    "    return np.stack([x**k for k in range(deg + 1)], axis=1)\n",
    "\n",
    "def fit_ridge(X, y, rho):\n",
    "    d = X.shape[1]\n",
    "    return np.linalg.solve(X.T @ X + rho * np.eye(d), X.T @ y)\n",
    "\n",
    "def kfold_cv(x, y, deg, rho, K=5):\n",
    "    n = len(x)\n",
    "    idx = np.arange(n)\n",
    "    np.random.shuffle(idx)\n",
    "    folds = np.array_split(idx, K)\n",
    "    scores = []\n",
    "    for k in range(K):\n",
    "        val_idx = folds[k]\n",
    "        tr_idx = np.concatenate([folds[j] for j in range(K) if j != k])\n",
    "        X_tr = poly_features(x[tr_idx], deg)\n",
    "        y_tr = y[tr_idx]\n",
    "        X_val = poly_features(x[val_idx], deg)\n",
    "        y_val = y[val_idx]\n",
    "        theta = fit_ridge(X_tr, y_tr, rho)\n",
    "        mse = np.mean((X_val @ theta - y_val)**2)\n",
    "        scores.append(mse)\n",
    "    return np.mean(scores)\n",
    "\n",
    "np.random.seed(99)\n",
    "n = 60\n",
    "x_data = np.random.uniform(-2, 3, n)\n",
    "y_data = h_star(x_data) + np.random.normal(0, 0.6, n)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Grid search over degree and rho using 5-fold CV\n",
    "degrees = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7]\n",
    "rhos = [0.0, 0.01, 0.1, 1.0, 10.0]\n",
    "results = {}\n",
    "for deg, rho in product(degrees, rhos):\n",
    "    results[(deg, rho)] = kfold_cv(x_data, y_data, deg, rho)\n",
    "\n",
    "best = min(results, key=results.get)\n",
    "print(f\"5-fold CV grid search over degree × ρ:\")\n",
    "print(f\"Best: degree={best[0]}, ρ={best[1]}, CV-MSE={results[best]:.4f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"\\nTrue function is degree-2 → CV correctly identified degree={best[0]}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Show CV scores as a table\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "table = pd.DataFrame(\n",
    "    {f'ρ={r}': [results[(d, r)] for d in degrees] for r in rhos},\n",
    "    index=[f'deg {d}' for d in degrees]\n",
    ").round(4)\n",
    "print(\"\\nCV-MSE table (lower is better):\")\n",
    "print(table.to_string())"
   ]
  },
